# ~/.zprofile — login shells only, read after ~/.zshenv and before ~/.zshrc.
#
# ⚠ ZSH DOES NOT READ ~/.profile, AND NOTHING HERE USED TO BRIDGE THE GAP.
# The shell module links .zshrc, .aliases, .p10k.zsh, .bashrc, .profile and the
# ~/.zsh.d/* files, and no .zprofile, .zlogin or .zshenv at all. bash reads
# ~/.profile for a login shell; zsh never does, under any circumstances.
#
# On a desktop this is usually invisible, because the display manager sources
# ~/.profile into the session and every terminal inherits it. That is a
# convention, not a guarantee, and it does not hold here: measured on this box
# 2026-09-12, the session PATH carried /opt/swayfx/bin and ~/.cargo/bin but NOT
# ~/.local/bin, which ~/.profile prepends, i.e. GDM never sourced it. It is the
# same root cause as the "GDM starts sway without a login shell" note in
# config/waybar/config, seen from the other side.
#
# Under WSL it is never invisible: wsl.exe launches the login shell directly, so
# there is no desktop session to inherit from and nothing sources ~/.profile at
# any point. Everything in it silently does not apply: XDG_*, $HOME/bin and
# $HOME/go/bin on PATH, the WSLg XCURSOR_SIZE/GDK_SCALE fixes (which are
# themselves WSL-only, so the file's one WSL block was dead exactly where it was
# needed), and the whole ~/.profile.local hook, which is where machine-local
# settings such as CA bundles live.
#
# ⚠ Sourced under `emulate sh`: ~/.profile is POSIX shell, shared with bash, and
# must not be reinterpreted with zsh's globbing and word-splitting rules.
#
# ⚠ The DF_PROFILE_SOURCED guard is what makes this safe to add on a machine
# where something ELSE already sourced ~/.profile. The file prepends to PATH
# unconditionally, so a second pass would duplicate every entry it adds. The
# sentinel is exported by ~/.profile itself rather than set here, which is the
# only version that can see a display manager's pass.
if [[ -r "$HOME/.profile" && -z "$DF_PROFILE_SOURCED" ]]; then
  emulate sh -c 'source "$HOME/.profile"'
fi
